  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 256 PM MDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nEnsemble guidance continues to be consistent with southwesterly flow \noverhead and troughing across the Western United States at 500-mb. \nThe trough is forecast to move across the Southern and Central \nUnited States Monday and Tuesday, and be located across the 4-\nCorners Region by Wednesday morning. However, ensembles continue to \nshow large amounts of spread on the trailing edge of the trough, \nwhich could suggest a slower eastward progression. Uncertainty \nregarding the feature's eastward movement becomes even more \nuncertain beyond Wednesday morning, when model guidance begins to \ndiverge significantly. \n\nAs troughing remains to the west of the forecast region on Monday, \nhigh temperatures are forecast to be warm in the upper 70s and 80s. \nHowever, an active jet streak to the north is expected to be linked \nwith a cold front approaching the forecast area. Precipitation is \nforecast along the front. NBM 24 hr precipitation guidance suggests \nthat most areas north and east of a line from Wray, CO to Leoti, KS \nhave a 40% chance or greater to receive 0.1 inches of rain or more \nMonday. These chances increase to as high as 80% in portions of Red \nWillow County. Additionally, some of this activity could be \nassociated with thunderstorms, as LREF guidance continues to show \nscattered areas where chances for surface CAPE (a measure of \natmospheric instability) greater than 1000 J/kg are 1 in 3. The same \nguidance shows around a 30-45% chance for sfc-500mb wind shear \ngreater than 30 kts across most of the forecast area except some \nareas of Eastern Colorado and far Southwest Nebraska. As such, a \nsevere storm or two may be in play, but widespread rain seems to be \nthe more likely scenario. Confidence in severe weather occurring \nMonday afternoon or evening is around 5%. \n\nCool and wet conditions are forecast Tuesday as the cold front \ntraverses the region and troughing from the west continues to \napproach. High temperatures are forecast in the low to mid 70s \nTuesday. NBM 24 hr precipitation guidance also shows all areas in a \n50% chance or higher for greater than 0.1 inches of rain Tuesday, \nexcept portions of Northern Yuma County in Colorado and Northwest \nDundy County in Nebraska. This activity is not favored to be related \nto thunderstorms, as LREF guidance shows all areas at less than a \n20% chance for greater than 1000 J/kg of surface CAPE. \n\nAgain, eastward progression of the trough from the west becomes most \nuncertain beyond Wednesday morning. However, guidance continues to \nshow a cyclonically curved jet streak around the base of the trough, \nwhich would favor a deep surface low at the leading edge of the \ntrough and in the left exit region of the jet. This low may lift \ninto Central Kansas throughout the day Wednesday, and increase the \nlikelihood for precipitation and strong northerly winds. Current \nforecast temperatures for Wednesday are in the upper 60s to mid 70s, \nwith NBM precipitation guidance still showing much of the area at a \n50% chance or higher for greater than 0.1 inches of rain. Even so, \ntemperatures may be allowed to drop further due to precipitation and \nnortherly wind gusts forecast up to 35 mph. Additionally, EC \ndeterministic and ensemble guidance seems to stall this trough the \nmost, which would support continued rain and cool temperatures for \nthe remainder of the forecast period. This could pose a risk for \nflash flooding, most particularly Wednesday and Thursday when higher \npercentile scenarios from the NBM show between 1 and 2 inches of \nrain both days. However, this continues to be a low confidence \nscenario, at around 5%. If the trough is able to move through the \narea quicker, similar to the mean GEFS solution, warm and dry \nconditions may be allowed to return Thursday and Friday.",
